Define the  GLOBAL indices of the wavevector q as (nx,ny,nz), i.e.

    q = (2*pi*nx/Lx,2*pi*ny/Ly,2*pi*nz/Lz)

with Lx, Ly, and Lz being the system sizes in real space. If the
array is arr(nx,ny,nz) then the correct symmetry is

   conjugate(array(nx,ny,nz)) = array(kx*Nx-nx,ky*Ny-ny,kz*Nz-nz)

where Nx, Ny, Nz are the grid point numbers, and kx, ky, and kz
are all (arbitrary) integers. 

First, we note that the box in Fourier space chosen by DFT is
not the first Brillouin zone. Below, we show the Fourier space
domain used in DFT for a c2r array with size (Nx//2+1) x Ny x Nz.


    Nx//2 ________________________
         /                       /|
        /                       / | 
    0  /______________________ /  |
  Ny-1 |                       |  |
       |                       |  |
       |                       |  |
       |                       |  |
       |                       |  |
       |                       |  |
       |                       |  /
       |                       | /
       |_______________________|/
     0
       0                      Nz-1


The planes nx=0 and nx=Nx//2 have redundant information in them,
all other points are unique under the symmetry. Therefore, the
relevant case for us is

   conjugate(array(0,ny,nz)) = array(0,ky*Ny-ny,kz*Nz-nz).

and, if Nx is an even number,

   conjugate(array(Nx//2,ny,nz)) = array(Nx//2,ky*Ny-ny,kz*Nz-nz)

for c2r arrays.

In order to proceed, we split both the nx=0 and the nx=Nx//2 (if Nx
is even) planes into four blocks:


        _______________________
 Ny-1  |            |          |
       |    ^       |   ^      |
       |    C->     |   D->    |
       |____________|__________|
 Ny//2 |            |          |
       |    ^       |   ^      |
       |    A ->    |   B->    |
       |            |          |
       |____________|__________|
     0
       0          Nz//2       Nz-1

For a single processor, the symmetry is fairly straightforward to
enforce. We choose block A to pass information to Block D, and Block C
to pass information to block B. The additional complication is that
these planes are split by cpu processor as well. So one needs to send
and receive data across processors.

Single processor:

We consider the nx = 0 plane here (everything generalises to nx=Nx//2
for even Nx also).


Case 1: Ny % 2 == 0 and Nz % 2 == 0.

There are four points in the plane that are purely real:

(0,0,0)
(0,Ny//2,0)
(0,0,Nz//2)
(0,Ny//2,Nz//2)


There are then also four lines which have the symmetry

   conjugate(array(0,0,nz)) = array(0,0,Nz-nz)

   conjugate(array(0,Ny//2,nz)) = array(0,Ny//2,Nz-nz)

   conjugate(array(0,ny,0)) = array(0,Ny-ny,0)

   conjugate(array(0,ny,Nz//2)) = array(0,Ny-ny,Nz//2)

Finally, the remaining points within the blocks must be filled.
We decide to always send from A and C to receive at D and B.

for (int nz = 1; nz < array.global_Nz()//2; nz++) {

    for (int ny = 1; ny < array.global_Ny()//2; ny++) {
    
       // currently in Block A
       
       array(0,ny,nz) = PHI(0,ny,nz); // whatever function
       array(0,Ny-ny,Nz-nz) = conjugate(array(0,ny,nz));

   for (int ny = array.global_Ny()//2+1; ny ++) {
   
       // now in Block C, must shift the calculation of PHI
       // so that we stay in the first Brillouin zone
       
       array(0,ny,nz) = PHI(0,ny-Ny,nz); // SECOND ARGUMENT SHIFTED
       array(0,Ny-ny,Nz-nz) = conjugate(array(0,ny,nz));

   }

}



Case 2: Ny % 2 == 0 and Nz % 2 == 1.

There are now only two points in the plane that are purely real:

(0,0,0)
(0,Ny//2,0)


There are then only three lines which have the symmetry

   conjugate(array(0,0,nz)) = array(0,0,Nz-nz)

   conjugate(array(0,Ny//2,nz)) = array(0,Ny//2,Nz-nz)

   conjugate(array(0,ny,0)) = array(0,Ny-ny,0)


Finally, the remaining points within the blocks must be filled.
We decide to always send from A and C to receive at D and B.

The loop here looks the same, but since the number Nz is odd
and integer division rounds down, it does something different
at Nz//2 and Nz//2+1.


for (int nz = 1; nz < array.global_Nz()//2; nz++) {

    for (int ny = 1; ny < array.global_Ny()//2; ny++) {
    
       // currently in Block A
       
       array(0,ny,nz) = PHI(0,ny,nz); // whatever function
       array(0,Ny-ny,Nz-nz) = conjugate(array(0,ny,nz));

   for (int ny = array.global_Ny()//2+1; ny ++) {
   
       // now in Block C, must shift the calculation of PHI
       // so that we stay in the first Brillouin zone
       
       array(0,ny,nz) = PHI(0,ny-Ny,nz); // SECOND ARGUMENT SHIFTED
       array(0,Ny-ny,Nz-nz) = conjugate(array(0,ny,nz));

   }

}


Case 3: Ny % 2 == 1 and Nz % 2 == 0.

There are again only two points in the plane that are purely real:

(0,0,0)
(0,0,Nz//2)


There are then only three lines which have the symmetry

   conjugate(array(0,0,nz)) = array(0,0,Nz-nz)

   conjugate(array(0,ny,0)) = array(0,Ny-ny,0)

   conjugate(array(0,ny,Nz//2)) = array(0,Ny-ny,Nz//2)

Finally, the remaining points within the blocks must be filled.
We decide to always send from A and C to receive at D and B.

The loop here is slightly different in that it loops over
all ny values (not skipping a middle value as in the case of
even Ny).

for (int nz = 1; nz < array.global_Nz()//2; nz++) {

    for (int ny = 1; ny < array.global_Ny()//2; ny++) {
    
       // currently in Block A
       
       array(0,ny,nz) = PHI(0,ny,nz); // whatever function
       array(0,Ny-ny,Nz-nz) = conjugate(array(0,ny,nz));

   for (int ny = array.global_Ny()//2; ny ++) {
   
       // now in Block C, must shift the calculation of PHI
       // so that we stay in the first Brillouin zone
       
       array(0,ny,nz) = PHI(0,ny-Ny,nz); // SECOND ARGUMENT SHIFTED
       array(0,Ny-ny,Nz-nz) = conjugate(array(0,ny,nz));

   }

}


Case 4: Ny % 2 == 1 and Nz % 2 == 1.

There is only one point in the plane that is purely real:

(0,0,0)


There are then only two lines which have the symmetry

   conjugate(array(0,0,nz)) = array(0,0,Nz-nz)

   conjugate(array(0,ny,0)) = array(0,Ny-ny,0)

Finally, the remaining points within the blocks must be filled.
We decide to always send from A and C to receive at D and B.

The loop is just a combination of cases 2 and 3 above.

for (int nz = 1; nz < array.global_Nz()//2; nz++) {

    for (int ny = 1; ny < array.global_Ny()//2; ny++) {
    
       // currently in Block A
       
       array(0,ny,nz) = PHI(0,ny,nz); // whatever function
       array(0,Ny-ny,Nz-nz) = conjugate(array(0,ny,nz));

   for (int ny = array.global_Ny()//2; ny ++) {
   
       // now in Block C, must shift the calculation of PHI
       // so that we stay in the first Brillouin zone
       
       array(0,ny,nz) = PHI(0,ny-Ny,nz); // SECOND ARGUMENT SHIFTED
       array(0,Ny-ny,Nz-nz) = conjugate(array(0,ny,nz));

   }

}
